{"code":"MC-DUPLICATE-KEY","definition":"The same mapping key is declared more than once in one\nscope of a decision file (YAML 1.2 §3.2.1 requires the\nkeys of a mapping to be unique).","occurs_when":"`mathc validate` walks the parsed document\n(`lib/codec.ml::duplicate_keys`) and finds a key bound\ntwice in one mapping. The kernel's readers\n(`lib/schema.ml::take_string` / `take_array` /\n`take_object` / `take_number`) all resolve a key with\n`List.assoc_opt`, which returns the FIRST binding — so\nthe later block is not overridden, it is UNREACHABLE:\nits content is in the tree and no reader can ever see\nit.\n\nThe severity is Warn because the kernel still produces a\nwell-typed decision from the surviving block. For a\nduplicated `obligations:` key that means the dropped\nobligations never reach `mathc gate` or `mathc\nself-check`, so a verdict can be `pass` about promises\nthat were never checked — the diagnostic is the only\nsignal that the file says more than the gate read.","remediation":"Merge the declarations into one. Two `obligations:`\nblocks become one list holding every obligation from\nboth, in the order you want them checked; two\n`counterexample:` blocks become one `|` block whose\ntext contains both halves. Do NOT delete the second\nblock to silence the warning — that deletes the\npromises the author wrote. After editing, recompute\n`body_sha` / `yaml_sha` (`lib/decision.ml::\nbody_canonical_sha256`, spec/algebra-3.2.md §7.1) and\nbump `revision` if the file declares one, then re-run\n`mathc validate <file>`.\n\nSee `decisions/duplicate-mapping-keys-silent-drop-\n2026-10.yaml` for the corpus that motivated the code."}
{"code":"MC-UNPARSED-TAIL","definition":"The kernel's YAML loader stopped reading the document\nbefore the end of the file, and `Codec.load_yaml_string`\ndiscarded the remaining lines without saying so.","occurs_when":"`mathc validate` calls\n`lib/codec.ml::load_yaml_string_full`, which keeps the\ntoken list that `parse_yaml_pairs` did NOT consume. A\nnon-empty remainder means the parser met a token deeper\nthan the mapping it was closing and returned early\n(`parse_yaml_pairs`, the `head_indent tokens > cur_indent`\nclause). The verdict is still `parse-ok` — the prefix that\nWAS read usually types fine — so without this diagnostic\na decision can be missing its entire `obligations:`,\n`risk:`, `relations:` and `reversal:` tail while the gate\nreports it as fully checked.\n\nThe corpus reached this through `fold_continuation`, whose\n\"a deeper line containing a colon is a nested key, stop\nfolding\" heuristic misfires on prose: English sentences\ncontain colons (`Mitigation: rename the install step`).\n\nThis is a different mechanism from `MC-DUPLICATE-KEY`.\nA duplicated key is IN the tree and unreachable; an\nunparsed tail is NOT in the tree at all. Fixing the\nduplicate does not restore the tail, and vice versa.","remediation":"Look at the line the diagnostic names. Put the prose in a\n`|` block scalar (`consequence_if_false: |`) so the\ncontinuation is read as text rather than guessed at, or\nrewrap the plain scalar so no continuation line carries a\ncolon. Both preserve the wording. Re-run\n`mathc validate <file>` — the file must print `parse-ok`\nwith no `MC-UNPARSED-TAIL` line on stderr.\n\nThe parser-side fix for `fold_continuation` itself is\ntracked as a follow-up decision; see\n`decisions/duplicate-mapping-keys-silent-drop-2026-10.yaml`."}
