{"code":"MC-SHA-DIGEST-STALE","definition":"A Decision's `body_sha` and `yaml_sha` agree with each\nother, and both are wrong: their shared value is not the\ndigest of the file they live in. Spec/algebra-3.2.md §7.1\ndefines `body_sha` as the sha256 of the file's own bytes\nwith both digest fields replaced by the placeholder\n`sha256:` + 64 zeros, so an edit to the body that was not\naccompanied by a recompute leaves the stored value stale.\n\nThis is the check that carries the tamper-evidence claim\nin `adoption/SKILL.md`. Without it, a decision body could\nbe rewritten under a digest that still asserted the old\ncontent, and every downstream verdict still read `pass`.\nEqual-but-wrong is why it is a separate code and not a\nreuse of `MC-SHA-MISMATCH`: the 64-zero placeholder\nemitted by `mathc new-decision` satisfies the equality\ninvariant forever, which is precisely this failure.","occurs_when":"`lib/decision.ml::sha_match_check` is given the file's raw\nbytes and recomputes the digest, then finds it differs from\nthe stored `body_sha`. `bin/Mathc.ml::validate_with_counts`\npasses the bytes it already read for parsing, so the\nvalidate path cannot silently fall back to the weaker\nstring-only check. Verdict `reject`, exit code 1.\n\n`mathc self-check` recomputes it too, for every\ndecision, and reports the subject as `fail` (exit 1). It\npreviously read the attestation store without looking at the\ndecision bytes, so a tampered decision came back `unknown`\n- indistinguishable from one that merely lacked an\nattestation. `load_decision_entry` already keeps the raw text\nin `Memory.source`, so no extra I/O was needed; the verdict\nis forced to Block AFTER waiver aggregation, so a stale\ndigest cannot be waived away.\n\nKNOWN GAP: `mathc gate` does not yet look at the digest.\nIt has the bytes (`Gate.evaluate` receives `Memory.t`,\nwhose entries carry `Memory.source`) but does not check\nthem. Recorded in\n`decisions/enforce-body-sha-digest-2026-10`.","remediation":"Run `mathc rehash <path> --write`. It recomputes both\nfields from the file's own bytes, verifies each stamped\nfile is a fixed point (re-hashing the result must\nreproduce the digest) before writing, and refuses to write\na file that fails that check. Dry-run by default: omit\n`--write` to see what would change. Then re-run\n`mathc validate`."}
